 that implies the printed one. Lean status per paper is kept in authored/evidence-status.json.","citation_ids":["cite.long.lem-rank-step-trichotomy"],"evidence_ids":["mapping.long.lem-rank-step-trichotomy"],"id":"long.lem-rank-step-trichotomy","kind":"claim","label":"Long record Lemma 6.36 (The two next-prefix intervals and their gap)","locator":"Long record Lemma 6.36, p. 54, label lem:rank-step-trichotomy","source_id":"paper.long.pdf","statement":"*Let $d\\ge0$ be an integer, let $u\\subseteq\\{1,\\ldots,d\\}$, and suppose the target $t$ lies in the interval $[X_u(2),X_u(2)+R_d]$. At depth $d+1$, either $t$ lies in the lower interval\n$$\n[X_u(2),X_u(2)+R_{d+1}],\n$$\nor in the upper interval\n$$\n[X_u(2)+w_{d+1},X_u(2)+w_{d+1}+R_{d+1}],\n$$\nor in the open gap between them. These alternatives are disjoint because $R_{d+1}<w_{d+1}$, and exhaustive because $R_d=w_{d+1}+R_{d+1}$. For $t=1/2$, Lemma 6.38 also excludes the interval endpoints, so all relevant comparisons are strict. Endpoint exclusion is not what makes the two child intervals disjoint; the strict-tail inequality does that.*","status":"unverified"},"ref":"sha256:264b08973a5f23b2f98ad989b0c1708096513d86929f19048623a2a9fe18092c/nodes/long.lem-rank-step-trichotomy","relations":[{"derived":false,"family":"arguments","from":"sha256:264b08973a5f23b2f98ad989b0c1708096513d86929f19048623a2a9fe18092c/nodes/long.lem-rank-step-trichotomy","id":"sha256:264b08973a5f23b2f98ad989b0c1708096513d86929f19048623a2a9fe18092c/edges/%5B%22long.lem-rank-step-trichotomy%22%2C%22lean.Erdos249257.IsStraddlePrefix.half_step_forced%22%2C%22formalizes%22%5D","kind":"formalizes","record_ref":"sha256:264b08973a5f23b2f98ad989b0c1708096513d86929f19048623a2a9fe18092c/edges/%5B%22long.lem-rank-step-trichotomy%22%2C%22lean.Erdos249257.IsStraddlePrefix.half_step_forced%22%2C%22formalizes%22%5D","semantic_class":"formalization_correspondence","to":"sha256:264b08973a5f23b2f98ad989b0c1708096513d86929f19048623a2a9fe18092c/nodes/lean.Erdos249257.